4.3 Step-writing guidelines
1. One action per step. Keep steps short and imperative ("Click Submit", not "The user should click Submit").
2. Every verification gets an expected result. Mark verification steps explicitly; ADO attaches pass/fail to each step.
3. State preconditions in the Summary, or use a Shared Step for setup (login, data seeding).
4. Use Shared Steps for repeated flows. Update once and all linked cases inherit the change.
5. Use Parameters (@username, @expectedMessage) with a Parameter Values table for data-driven cases instead of duplicating cases.
6. Be specific and measurable. Avoid "works correctly"; state the exact message, value, or state.
7. Attach supporting artifacts (sample files, screenshots, API payloads) to the test case.
8. Keep cases independent where possible; avoid dependence on another case's outcome.
4.4 Coverage expectations per story
For each User Story, write at minimum:
• Positive (happy path) case(s) for each acceptance criterion
• Negative cases (invalid input, unauthorized access, missing data)
• Boundary / edge cases
• At least one regression-tagged case for core behavior
• API / integration cases where services are involved
